---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title = 'About World Battery Hub';
const description = 'About World Battery Hub — a battery knowledge and data platform. Editorial method, source hierarchy, the Research Desk process, correction policy, editorial independence, and operator disclosure.';
const canonical = '/about/';
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  ogType="website"
  dateModified="2026-10-23"
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="byline">About · Last reviewed 2026-10-23</p>
      <h1>About World Battery Hub</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        World Battery Hub is a battery knowledge and data platform. It is informational and educational —
        not a store, not a certification body, and not a laboratory. This page explains what the platform is,
        how its content is made, how errors are handled, how its commercial section is kept separate from its
        editorial content, and who operates it.
      </p>

      <div class="takeaways">
        <strong>At a glance:</strong> World Battery Hub is a battery knowledge and data platform — six sites
        covering <strong>knowledge</strong> (lead-acid, lithium, basics, tools),
        <strong>data</strong> (battery groups, references), <strong>technology</strong> (engineering),
        <strong>testing</strong> (methods and standards), <strong>manufacturers</strong> and
        <strong>market intelligence</strong>. Content is researched from public sources, cross-verified, and
        labelled with its sources, standards and limitations. We do not fabricate figures, and we do not
        publish a legal-entity name until the operator has confirmed it.
      </div>

      <h2>What this platform is</h2>
      <p>
        World Battery Hub is a reference platform for people who work with batteries — engineers, buyers,
        importers, distributors, students and researchers. It explains how battery chemistries and ratings
        work, publishes specification and comparison data, offers calculators and tools, and documents
        standards, manufacturers and market figures.
      </p>
      <p>
        The platform has two clearly separated parts. The <strong>editorial and data</strong> part is what
        this About page describes: researched, cross-verified, source-labelled reference content. The
        <strong>commercial</strong> part is the <a href="/lead-acid/products/">lead-acid product catalogue</a>,
        which operates on a quotation (RFQ) basis and is kept visibly separate from editorial content. Nothing
        in the editorial part is paid placement, and nothing in the commercial part is presented as editorial
        judgement.
      </p>

      <h2>What the platform covers</h2>
      <p>The platform spans six areas, each maintained as its own site or section:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/lead-acid/"><strong>Lead-acid knowledge</strong></a> — battery groups, <a href="/lead-acid/cca/what-is-cca/">CCA</a>, <a href="/lead-acid/agm-battery/">AGM</a>/<a href="/lead-acid/efb-battery/">EFB</a>/gel/flooded chemistries, applications, charging and maintenance.</li>
        <li><a href="/lithium/"><strong>Lithium knowledge</strong></a> — chemistries, cells, <a href="/lithium/what-is-bms/">BMS</a>, safety and emerging technology.</li>
        <li><a href="/basics/"><strong>Battery Basics 101</strong></a> — the beginner path from "what is a battery" through capacity, voltage, charging and safety.</li>
        <li><a href="/tools/"><strong>Calculators and tools</strong></a> — runtime, sizing, charging-time and battery-finder tools.</li>
        <li><a href="/standards/"><strong>Standards</strong></a> — the standards directory used to interpret ratings.</li>
        <li><a href="/lead-acid/products/"><strong>Product catalogue</strong></a> — the commercial section, clearly separated from the rest.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Editorial method</h2>
      <p>
        Every piece of editorial content is produced by the same process and to the same standard. The method
        is stated so a reader — human or machine — can see, before trusting a number, how it was obtained.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Cross-verified, not translated.</strong> Content is synthesized from multiple independent
            sources, including Chinese and English primary material, rather than translated or paraphrased
            from a single source.</li>
        <li><strong>Every number carries its standard.</strong> A <a href="/lead-acid/cca/what-is-cca/">CCA</a>
            value states whether it is JIS, EN, SAE or DIN; a capacity states its rate (C20, C5, and so on);
            a dimension states its group convention. A number without a standard is not presented as fact.</li>
        <li><strong>Conflicts are explained, not hidden.</strong> Where sources disagree, the disagreement and
            its likely cause are stated rather than a single number being silently chosen.</li>
        <li><strong>No fabrication.</strong> If a figure cannot be traced to a source, it is stated
            qualitatively or marked as unknown. A missing value is left as a gap, never estimated to fill it.</li>
        <li><strong>Confidence is labelled.</strong> Where a value is a typical range, a standard-defined limit
            or a manufacturer claim, the page says so, so a representative range is not mistaken for a
            guarantee.</li>
        <li><strong>Limitations are stated.</strong> Calculators and worked examples are illustrative models
            with simplified assumptions; they are not a substitute for a design review or a load study.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Source hierarchy</h2>
      <p>Sources are ranked by independence and authority, in this order:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Primary</strong> — manufacturer documentation, standards bodies (SAE, EN, JIS, IEC, UL,
            UN), and government or regulatory data.</li>
        <li><strong>Secondary</strong> — industry publications, trade organisations and technical reviews.</li>
        <li><strong>Community</strong> — forums and user reports, used only to discover real-world questions,
            never as evidence.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        A claim is asserted as fact only when it can be traced to a primary or a corroborated secondary source.
        Anything below that threshold is stated qualitatively, or marked as unknown. Community sources are
        used to find out what people actually ask, not to prove what is true.
      </p>

      <h2 id="research-desk">The World Battery Hub Research Desk</h2>
      <p>
        Content on this platform is produced by the <strong>World Battery Hub Research Desk</strong> — a
        collective editorial entity, not a named individual. We deliberately do not present a single-author
        "expert" persona, because a fabricated expert would be worse than an honest collective: the desk's
        authority rests on its <em>process</em>, which is stated and checkable, not on a name.
      </p>
      <p>The desk's production process runs in four steps, in order:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Collect</strong> — gather primary material: manufacturer datasheets, standards documents,
            and government or industry data.</li>
        <li><strong>Cross-check</strong> — reconcile at least two independent sources for any factual claim,
            and record where they agree.</li>
        <li><strong>Label</strong> — attach the source, the test standard and the confidence level to every
            number before publication.</li>
        <li><strong>Explain</strong> — where sources disagree, state the disagreement and its likely cause
            instead of silently choosing one value.</li>
      </ol>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Research methodology</strong> — synthesis from multiple independent sources, cross-verified,
            never translated from a single source. See the full <a href="/methodology/">methodology</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Source verification</strong> — every figure carries its source and its test standard; a
            number without a traceable source is stated qualitatively or marked unknown.</li>
        <li><strong>Technical review</strong> — claims are reconciled against standards documents and
            manufacturer datasheets before publication; conflicts are explained, not silently resolved.</li>
        <li><strong>Correction policy</strong> — errors are corrected in place and logged with the date, the
            old value and the new value.</li>
        <li><strong>Editorial independence</strong> — the commercial product catalogue is separated from
            editorial and data content, and a commercial relationship does not change a rating, a standard or
            a confidence level.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If a real engineer or researcher reviews this material in the future, that person will be credited as a
        named reviewer — a real <a href="/methodology/">Person entity</a> with their own record — never as a
        fictional byline.
      </p>

      <h2>Correction policy</h2>
      <p>
        Errors are corrected in place and logged. A correction does not rewrite history silently: the change is
        recorded with the date, the original value and the corrected value, so a reader can see what changed
        and when. Material corrections — those that change a specification, a standard reference or a
        conclusion — are reflected in the page's review date.
      </p>
      <p>
        To report an error, use the <a href="/contact/#editorial">editorial correction route</a> on the
        Contact page. Include the page URL, the current value and the corrected value (with a source if you
        have one). Reports are triaged against primary sources before any change is made.
      </p>

      <h2>Editorial independence</h2>
      <p>
        The platform has a commercial section (the <a href="/lead-acid/products/">product catalogue</a>) that is
        clearly separated from the editorial and data content. Editorial conclusions, buyer guidance and stance
        blocks are the Research Desk's own analysis and are labelled as such. They are not paid placement, and
        a commercial relationship — present or prospective — does not change a rating, a standard, a confidence
        level or a recommendation.
      </p>
      <p>
        We do not accept payment to rank a manufacturer, to inflate a specification, or to suppress a finding.
        Where a figure comes from a manufacturer's own claim, it is labelled as a manufacturer claim and is not
        upgraded to "verified" without independent evidence.
      </p>

      <h2 id="operator-disclosure">Operator and platform disclosure</h2>
      <p>
        World Battery Hub is a battery knowledge and data platform. It publishes editorial and data content, and
        operates a clearly separated commercial product catalogue with a quotation (RFQ) process. This section
        states, honestly and without fabrication, what is and is not yet disclosed about the entity that
        operates the platform.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Operating entity — pending operator confirmation.</strong> The legal entity that operates this
        platform — its registered legal name, registered address, registration identifier (unified social
        credit code or equivalent) and legal representative — has not yet been published on this site. We do
        not invent a legal name, address or registration number, because an incorrect or fabricated legal
        entity would be worse than a clearly stated pending field.
      </p>
      <p>
        Until these fields are confirmed, the platform's legal identity is described as "the operator of World
        Battery Hub", and contact is made through the channels listed on the Contact page.
      </p>
      <p><strong>Pending fields</strong> — to be added once confirmed by the operator:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Registered legal name of the operating entity.</li>
        <li>Registered address (country, and full address where applicable).</li>
        <li>Registration identifier / unified social credit code (or equivalent).</li>
        <li>Contact telephone number.</li>
        <li>Final, non-placeholder contact email addresses.</li>
      </ul>

